 <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 rounded-r-xl p-6 mb-8">
 <p class="text-lg font-black uppercase text-amber-700 mb-3">Key Takeaways</p>
 <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm mb-4 speakable">PD 3.1 extends delivery to 240W via EPR at 28-48V, requiring 50V/5A E-Marker cables: PD 3.1 + PPS is the 2026 minimum spec for multi-port chargers, with 140W laptop models shipping now.</p>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>USB-C PD 3.1 extends power delivery from 100W (PD 3.0) to 240W</strong> via EPR (Extended Power Range) at 28V, 36V, and 48V. SPR (Standard Power Range) still covers up to 100W at 20V. For OEM buyers: PD 3.1 EPR is required for 140W+ laptop chargers; PD 3.0 SPR remains sufficient for phones, tablets, and sub-100W laptops.</li>
 <li><strong>EPR cables are physically different from SPR cables</strong>. EPR requires a certified E-Marker chip rated for 50V/5A (vs 20V/5A for SPR). Using an SPR cable with a 140W EPR charger limits output to 100W. OEM buyers: specify EPR-rated E-Marker chips (Cypress CYPD2104 or equivalent) in your BOM for any 100W+ charger SKU.</li>
 <li><strong>PD 3.1 + PPS is the 2026 minimum viable spec for multi-port chargers</strong>. A 140W PD 3.1 charger with PPS dynamically allocates power across ports (e.g., 100W to laptop + 30W to phone + 10W to earbuds). Without PPS, the charger uses fixed voltage rails and can't optimize per-device, resulting in slower charging and customer complaints about "my charger says 140W but my phone charges slow."</li>
 <li><strong>PD 3.1 adoption timeline for OEM planning</strong>: 140W laptop chargers are shipping now (MacBook Pro 16" is the reference platform). 180W-240W multi-port desktop chargers are ramping in H2 2026. 240W single-port chargers are still niche (limited device support). For 2026 OEM product lines, target 65W-140W PD 3.1 with PPS as your core SKU range.</li>
 </ul>
