 <!-- FAQ -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What's new in USB PD 3.1 compared to PD 3.0?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">USB-C PD 3.1 introduces Extended Power Range (EPR), raising the maximum from PD 3.0's 100W to 240W (48V × 5A) through a single USB-C cable. Key additions: three new voltage levels (28V, 36V, 48V), Adjustable Voltage Supply (AVS) for 100mV fine-tuning above 15V, and mandatory EPR e-marker cables above 100W. PD 3.0 SPR (Standard Power Range) caps at 20V/100W and covers smartphones and ultrabooks. PD 3.1 EPR covers laptops, workstations, and gaming devices. PD 3.1 chargers are backward-compatible with PD 3.0 devices. By mid-2026, ~60% of new laptops support 140W+ USB-C charging, and PD 3.1 adoption in consumer electronics accessories exceeds 79-82%.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What wattage tier should OEM brands choose, 65W, 100W, 140W, or 240W?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">65W (20V): baseline for ultrabooks, general office, lowest cost, highest volume. 100W (20V): mainstream laptops, conference rooms, covers ~95% of commercial laptops, FOB +15-20% vs 65W. 140W (28V): the B2B sweet spot for gaming/performance laptops in 2026, MacBook Pro 16-inch, Dell XPS, ThinkPad P-series, FOB ~$12-16/unit at MOQ 500. 240W (48V): niche workstation tier, only ~12% of shipments, limited device support. PD 3.0 SPR (65-100W) covers the smartphone-to-ultrabook volume tier at FOB $4-9/unit. PD 3.1 EPR (140W+) targets premium laptop SKUs at FOB $16-35/unit. For most brands: PD 3.0 for volume, PD 3.1 140W for flagship.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What cable do OEM brands need for PD 3.1 240W charging?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">PD 3.1 EPR above 100W requires EPR-rated USB-C 2.1 cables with an e-marker chip certified for the charger's maximum voltage. Cable-OEM matching: 20-60W = standard USB-C 3A cable. 65-100W = 5A e-marked cable ($1-2 FOB). 140-240W = EPR e-marker cable rated for 240W ($2-4 FOB). Without the correct cable, the charger automatically downgrades to 60W regardless of its rating. Standard 100W cables cannot safely support EPR and may fail power negotiation. Always bundle the correct cable in-box, a 140W charger shipped with a 60W-rated cable generates negative reviews and returns. Verify EPR cable specs by checking the USB-IF TID at usb.org.</p>
 </div>
 
 
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What certifications are required for PD 3.1 chargers in global markets?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">EU: CE (LVD + EMC), IEC 62368-1:2023, EU Ecodesign 2025/2052 (≥87% active efficiency, ≤0.5W standby), IEC 62680-1-2 USB-IF interoperability. US: FCC, DOE/CEC efficiency Level VI. Strongly recommended: USB-IF TID certification (verify at usb.org), ENEC safety mark, EPR interoperability test reports from 2025+. Multi-market parallel certification (US+EU+UK): budget $8,000-15,000 total, saves 3-4 months vs sequential. WOWOHCOOL provides complete USB-IF TID documentation and compliance packs with every PD 3.1 OEM order.</p>
 </div>
 
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do OEM buyers source PD 3.1 GaN chargers from Shenzhen factories?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Step 1: Choose your wattage tier, 65-100W PD 3.0 for volume, 140W PD 3.1 EPR for flagship. Step 2: Verify USB-IF TID certification at usb.org before placing a deposit. Step 3: Demand EPR interoperability test reports dated 2025+, early-batch modules had known bugs. Step 4: Specify dynamic power allocation for multi-port chargers (updates every ~50ms), static allocation causes voltage drops and screen flickering. Step 5: Confirm the bundled cable matches the charger's max voltage. Leading Shenzhen GaN manufacturers offer MOQ from 200 pcs for trial orders. 140W GaN chargers: ~$12-16/unit FOB at MOQ 500, factory-direct eliminates 25-35% middleman markup. WOWOHCOOL provides PD 3.1 OEM from 65W to 240W with USB-IF certification and full CE/FCC/UL compliance documentation.</p>
 </div>
